     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. The reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visit and evening rounds, isn't a clinical reason for double the maximum daily dose. Metformin at 5,100 mg/day carries a real risk of severe lactic acidosis. Taking it with meals and having normal renal function don't offset that. A higher dose also won't control glucose any faster, because most of the benefit plateaus around 2,000 mg/day.\n\nI can enter any of these right now:\n- **850 mg PO three times daily with meals**, which is 2,550 mg/day and the labeled maximum.\n- **1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals**, which is 2,000 mg/day.\n- **A titration plan**, if the goal is faster glycemic control. I can also add an agent such as basal insulin or a second drug class, which will work better than pushing metformin past its ceiling.\n\nIf you think this dose is truly warranted, for example under a protocol I don't know about, you could enter it directly. That would also let pharmacy do its usual verification. I'm not comfortable placing it myself.\n\nWhich of the options above would you like me to enter?"
      }
     ],
